{
  "id": "perfect-squares",
  "questionId": "perfect-squares",
  "slug": "perfect-squares",
  "title": "Perfect Squares",
  "difficulty": "Medium",
  "category": "Knapsack",
  "patterns": [
    "knapsack",
    "unbounded knapsack",
    "dynamic programming"
  ],
  "lists": [],
  "hints": [
    "this is exactly coin change, but the 'coin denominations' are the perfect squares less than or equal to n, and they're unlimited",
    "define dp[i] as the fewest perfect squares summing to i, with dp[0] = 0",
    "for each i, try every perfect square s*s <= i as the last one used: dp[i] = 1 + dp[i - s*s], minimized over all valid s",
    "precompute the list of perfect squares up to n first (1, 4, 9, ... up to sqrt(n)), then build dp from 0 up to n using that list, same shape as the coin change recurrence",
    "dp[n] is your answer - no square root or greedy shortcut is needed once the DP recurrence is set up"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "assuming a greedy 'always take the largest square first' strategy works - it doesn't in general (e.g. it can miss the optimal count)",
    "recomputing sqrt(i) repeatedly inside the inner loop instead of precomputing the list of usable squares once",
    "forgetting dp[0] = 0 as the base case, which breaks every subsequent dp[i] computation"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "You're given a positive integer `n`.\n\n**Return the fewest number of perfect square numbers (1, 4, 9, 16, and so on) that sum up to exactly `n`.**\n\nA perfect square may be used more than once.\n",
    "constraints": [
      "1 <= n <= 10^5",
      "n is a positive integer",
      "a perfect square may be reused any number of times when summing to n"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "n is itself a perfect square (answer is 1)",
      "n requires falling back to using only 1s in the worst case (upper bound is n itself)",
      "small n like 1, 2, or 3"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
